---
title: "Migrating from Quarto"
---

If you already write `.tmd`, you are most of the way here. qmd-fast reads the same
source dialect Quarto does for the things this tool is about: prose, executable
cells, callouts, math, cross-references. The differences are deliberate and small
in number, and they all follow from one decision: **qmd-fast renders to HTML and
nothing else.** No PDF, no Word, no format matrix. That single constraint is what
buys the live preview loop, so the migration is less "port your project" and more
"flatten one config file and delete a few format-only keys."

This page is the short version: what carries over untouched, what you gain, what is
gone or different, and a checklist that ends by letting the tool find your leftover
Quarto keys for you.

## What stays the same

Your documents mostly do not change. The following all work exactly as you know
them:

- **The `.tmd` file itself**: YAML front matter in a `---` block, then Markdown.
- **Executable cells**: `{python}` and `{r}` fenced cells, with `#|` cell options
  (`echo`, `include`, `cache`, `label`, `fig-cap`, `code-fold`, and the rest). See
  [Executable content](code.tmd).
- **Callouts**: `::: {.callout-note}` and friends. The five kinds (`note`, `tip`,
  `warning`, `important`, `caution`) match Quarto, icons and all.
- **Math**: inline `$…$` and display `$$…$$`, rendered with KaTeX (server-side, so
  the browser only loads a stylesheet).
- **Cross-references**: `@fig-`, `@sec-`, `@eq-`, `@tbl-`, `@lst-` resolve to
  numbered links, and across chapters in a book.
- **Citations**: `[@key]` against a `bibliography:` `.bib` file, with an
  auto-generated references section.
- **Most front matter**: `title`, `subtitle`, `author`, `date`, `description`,
  `lang`, `categories`, `image`, `toc`, `format`, `theme`, `css`, `bibliography`,
  and the `execute:` block all carry over. The full set is in the
  [configuration reference](../reference/configuration.tmd).
- **Includes and shortcodes**: `{{< include partial.tmd >}}` works, as do
  `{{< embed deck.tmd >}}` and `{{< video clip.mp4 >}}`.

The mental rule: if a feature is about *content in a browser*, it almost certainly
carries over. If it is about *producing a non-HTML artifact*, it is gone (see below).

## What you gain

This is the reason to switch. None of these have a Quarto equivalent:

- **Block-incremental live preview.** On save, qmd-fast diffs the new document
  against the old by content-hash and pushes only the blocks that changed over a
  websocket. The page never reloads, your scroll holds, and a running `{js}` widget
  or canvas keeps its state. See [The live preview loop](preview.tmd).
- **Click-to-source.** Alt-click (Option-click on Mac) any block in the preview to
  jump to its exact source line in your editor, even across `{{< include >}}`d
  files. The preview is a read-only view of the source; the editor is the only
  place you type.
- **A warm kernel that never cold-starts.** The Jupyter kernel stays alive across
  edits. Change one cell and only that cell (plus anything downstream of it)
  re-runs; everything above reuses the kernel's existing state. An unchanged
  document on a fresh `build` replays cell outputs from the `_freeze/` cache without
  booting a kernel at all.

::: {.callout-tip}
## The payoff in one sentence

Quarto re-renders the whole document on every change because it has to produce a
finished file; qmd-fast keeps a live HTML view and patches it block by block, which
is only possible because HTML is the one and only target.
:::

## What is dropped or different

The trades are all consequences of being HTML-only and of a clean-break,
closed-vocabulary config. Knowing them up front saves confusion:

| Quarto | qmd-fast |
|---|---|
| Many output formats (PDF, Word, ePub, LaTeX, Typst, …) | **HTML only.** A `format: pdf`/`docx`/`typst` block is not honored (and not flagged, since `format:` sub-keys are owned by extensions, not linted): it just renders as a plain HTML page. Remove these blocks yourself. A future print track would render *from* the built HTML, never as a parallel format |
| `_metadata.yml` directory cascade | **No cascade.** Config is two levels: the root `_site.yml` sets defaults, a page's own front matter overrides them. Nothing merges in from intermediate directories |
| `csl:` selects a citation style | **`csl:` is recognized but ignored.** References always render in a single built-in IEEE style. The key is accepted so it does not warn, but it has no effect |
| Quarto tolerates unknown / extra keys | **Closed vocabulary.** Any key that is not a qmd-fast key warns with a "did you mean …" hint, including Quarto-only keys like `title-block-banner` or `site-url`. The document still renders, and every warning is located so you can click straight to it |
| reveal.js slide vocabulary | **A native deck engine.** Decks render on qmd-fast's own engine (offline, bundled, no CDN). Most authoring carries over (`. . .` pauses, `code-line-numbers`, fragments), but there is no reveal.js plugin ecosystem or `window.Reveal` API. See [Slides, books & sites](formats.tmd) |

::: {.callout-note}
## "Warns" does not mean "breaks"

The closed-vocabulary check only **warns**. A leftover Quarto key will not stop a
render; it shows up as a located diagnostic (clickable in the live preview, listed
by `qmd-fast check`). That is by design: it surfaces dead keys so you can delete
them, instead of silently doing nothing the way an ignored key does in a tolerant
parser.
:::

## Flattening `_quarto.yml` to `_site.yml`

The biggest single change is the project config. Quarto's nested
`project:` / `website:` / `book:` / `format:` shape is no longer parsed: its
top-level keys warn as unknown, and its nested values are ignored. qmd-fast uses a
**flat, native** schema, because being HTML-only removes the need for the
`format: html:` nesting entirely.

Here is a typical website config, before and after.

**Before** (`_quarto.yml`):

```yaml
project:
  type: website
  output-dir: _site

website:
  title: "My Site"
  navbar:
    left:
      - { text: Blog, href: blog.tmd }
      - { text: Projects, href: projects.tmd }
  page-footer:
    left: "© 2026 Me"
    right:
      - { icon: github, href: "https://github.com/me" }

format:
  html:
    toc: true
    theme: cosmo
```

**After** (`_site.yml`):

```yaml
title: "My Site"
output: _site
toc: true
theme: dark            # built-in light | dark, a .css/.scss, or an extension bundle

nav:
  - { text: Blog, href: blog.tmd }
  - { text: Projects, href: projects.tmd }

footer:
  left: "© 2026 Me"
  right:
    - { icon: github, href: "https://github.com/me" }
```

The mapping, key by key:

| `_quarto.yml` | `_site.yml` |
|---|---|
| `website: title:` | `title:` |
| `format: html: toc:` | `toc:` |
| `website: navbar: left:` | `nav:` (a bare list is the left side; use `{ left: [...], right: [...] }` for both) |
| `website: page-footer:` | `footer:` |
| `project: output-dir:` | `output:` |
| `project:` / `website:` / `book:` (the wrappers themselves) | **deleted** (any leftover wrapper warns as an unknown top-level key) |

A **book** is the same flat file with a `chapters:` list; its presence is what makes
the project a book (no `project: type: book`). One mapping subtlety: when you omit
`output:`, a book defaults its build directory to `_book` (a plain site defaults to
`_site`), matching Quarto. The full `_site.yml` key set, the icon names available for
nav/footer items, and the page-level `listing:` / `about:` blocks are all in the
[configuration reference](../reference/configuration.tmd).

Two keys that look project-wide but are **front-matter-only**: `bibliography:` and
`csl:` live in a page's `---` block, not in `_site.yml`. They are not in the site
config vocabulary, so putting them in `_site.yml` warns as an unknown config key.

::: {.callout-warning}
## Theme names are not portable

`theme:` takes qmd-fast's built-in `light` or `dark`, a path to a `.css`/`.scss`
file, or an installed `_extensions/<name>/` bundle. A Quarto Bootswatch name like
`cosmo` or `flatly` is not recognized; pick a built-in or point at your own CSS. A
qmd-fast theme is just a `:root` block of `--qmd-*` custom properties, so porting a
look is usually a small file rather than a framework.
:::

## Decks: same authoring, different engine

If you have reveal.js slide decks, the good news is that the authoring you actually
use carries over: `format: revealjs`, `##` for a slide and `#` for a section,
`. . .` pauses, `code-line-numbers` line-stepping, `::: {.incremental}` and
`.fragment` reveals, magic-move, auto-animate, and speaker view all work.

What does *not* carry over is anything that reaches into reveal.js itself: there is
no reveal plugin ecosystem, and no `window.Reveal` object to script against (the
native engine exposes `window.QmdDeck` instead). Decks are bundled and offline, with
no CDN dependency. The full deck story, including how a deck gets into a site via
`{{< embed >}}`, is in [Slides, books & sites](formats.tmd).

## Migration checklist

A practical order of operations for moving a project across:

1. **Rename the config.** `_quarto.yml` becomes `_site.yml`. (If you run
   `qmd-fast check` on a directory that still has `_quarto.yml` and no `_site.yml`,
   it prints a short migration breadcrumb, pointing you at `qmd-fast init` and the
   docs, and stops there without validating any keys.)
2. **Flatten it.** Lift the nested keys per the table above and delete the
   `project:` / `website:` / `book:` / `format:` wrappers.
3. **Fix the theme.** Swap any Bootswatch theme name for `light`, `dark`, a CSS
   file, or an extension bundle.
4. **Drop format-only front matter.** Remove anything that only made sense for a
   non-HTML target: `format: pdf`/`docx`/`typst` blocks, `pdf-engine`,
   `documentclass`, and the like. Keep `format: html` (or `revealjs` for decks).
5. **Accept the citation-style trade.** A `csl:` key is harmless (it will not warn)
   but does nothing; references render in the built-in IEEE style regardless.
6. **Forget about `_metadata.yml`.** There is no directory cascade. Move any
   defaults you relied on into `_site.yml` (project-wide) or into the individual
   pages' front matter.
7. **Run `qmd-fast check` to surface leftover Quarto keys.** This is the payoff of
   the closed vocabulary: every unknown *top-level* key, in `_site.yml` and in each
   page's front matter, is reported with a "did you mean …" hint and a source
   location. One thing it will *not* catch is a leftover `format:` sub-block (step 4):
   those sub-keys belong to extensions and are not linted, so a stray `format: pdf:`
   passes silently. Delete format blocks by hand.

```sh
qmd-fast check my-project            # human-readable: path:line: message
qmd-fast check my-project --format json   # machine-readable, for a script or CI
```

`check` renders the whole project in memory without executing any cells, so it is
fast and safe to run repeatedly. Work through its list until it reports zero
problems, and the migration is done. If you want a migration that *fails* on
leftovers (in CI, say), pair it with `qmd-fast build --strict`, which actually runs
cells and exits non-zero on a located warning, a broken cross-reference, or a crashed
cell. See the [CLI reference](../reference/cli.tmd) for every `check` and `build`
flag, and [Writing](writing.tmd) for the prose-side features once you are rendering
cleanly.
